  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe combination of some weak daytime instability and the upper\nlevel low/cold pool aloft will continue to result in some\nscattered showers through this evening, before becoming more\nisolated overnight. Low temperatures will be into the lower to\nmiddle 50s. Patchy fog is likely overnight, especially across\nthe valleys and in locations that received recent rainfall.\n\nLow pressure will exit well to our northeast on Friday as\nsurface high pressure builds to our south. This will allow for a\nbrighter and warmer day with high temperatures into the lower\n80s under partly cloudy skies. A few diurnally driven showers\ncannot be ruled out though as we remain under weak cyclonic\nflow. Friday night will feature dry conditions with low\ntemperatures into the 50s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAn upper level ridge axis will cross the region on Saturday which \nwill keep the area mostly dry, but a few isolated mountain \nshowers/storms will be possible. Temperatures will increase on \nSaturday with the ridge moving overhead, with highs expected to \nrange from the mid/upper 70s north, to the low/mid 80s elsewhere.  \n\nOn Sunday, we transition back into southwest flow aloft as the next \ntrough approaches slowly from the west. This will keep temperatures \nsimilar to Saturday but with abundant cloud cover in place, it could \nbe just a touch cooler. The next chance for widespread rainfall will \nthen move in late Sunday, potentially sticking around through Monday \nevening/night. As of now, this system doesn't look to be a prolific \nrainmaker with NBM probabilities of a half inch or greater only \nmaxing out in the 20 to 50 percent range, but most will see at least \nsome rain with the best chances in the foothill and mountains. After \nthis system exits, the rest of the week looks fairly quiet with \nnormal to above normal temperatures.",
